My Take on Undefined Behavior in C::title
<i>[David Priver], October 20th, 2026</i>
::raw
  <article>

A lot of digital ink has been spilled on the topic
of undefined behavior in C, what it means for the
programmer and unexpected trivia of what constitutes
undefined behavior in the C standard. It is good for a
C programmer to know what can cause undefined behavior,
but it seems to me there is a fundamental confusion
of what it means for behavior to be undefined by the C
standard.

To explain what I mean, we have to take a step back, and
understand what the C standard even is. The C standard
is a document specifying the requirements for a particular
implementation to be able to call itself an implementation
of the C programming language. That is it. That is all the
power it has. If the implementation doesn't follow the 
standard (to a fuzzy degree of completeness), it cannot
be called an implementation of the C programming language.

So given that, how does the standard define undefined
behavior?
::quote
  undefined behavior

  behavior, upon use of a nonportable or erroneous program
  construct or of erroneous data, for which this document
  imposes no requirements

Connecting that to what I just explained, undefined behavior
causes no particular constraint upon whether the
implementation is allowed to call itself an implementation
of the C programming language.  Being allowed to call itself
an implementation of the C programming language does not
free the implementation of other, possibly implied
requirements or from business requirements. 
One example of undefined behavior is that a non-empty
preprocessing unit has to end with a newline. If an
implementation upon finding that chooses to produce bad code,
it is still allowed to call itself an implementation of the
C programming language, but it's not a very useful one!

Somehow though, that definition of undefined behavior
has become the basis of a logical leap. The C standard
specifies requirements that an implementation must satisfy
to be conforming. It is not a logical framework for
understanding a C program. Undefined behavior is a situation
where the standard "imposes no requirements". Therefore the
document requires nothing about the semantics of the program
upon encountering undefined behavior. But that does not mean
the program has lost its semantics in some broader sense! An
implementation can choose to preserve meaningful,
predictable behavior in these situations, and in fact, that
is exactly what is suggested in the standard:
::quote
  Note 1 to entry: Possible undefined behavior ranges from
  ignoring the situation completely with unpredictable
  results, to behaving during translation or program
  execution in a documented manner characteristic of the
  environment (with or without the issuance of a diagnostic
  message), to terminating a translation or execution (with
  the issuance of a diagnostic message).

When a compiler chooses to exploit undefined behavior to
eliminate code or infer that certain execution paths are
impossible, that is a choice about how to implement C,
not an action mandated by the standard. Interpreting
undefined behavior as "this situation cannot happen" is a
choice, not required.

Some forms of undefined behavior are very common for
programmers to accidentally or unknowingly produce. Most C
programmers have not read the standard or have an
encyclopedic knowledge of it and so don't know that things
like signed integer overflow has behavior that is undefined.
A compiler developer is certainly allowed to exploit this to
say "as this behavior is undefined, I am allowed to optimize
as it cannot happen" and still call itself an implementation
of the C programming language. But if this choice introduces
security issues or just simply erroneous results, then it
is a less useful implementation of the C programming
language for its users than it could be. Something being
undefined behavior is not a blank check to do whatever you
want as a compiler developer. You still need to produce
a useful piece of software for your users. That is not to
say that the usage of undefined behavior to optimize is
always bad or wrong: it just needs to be weighed and decided
whether it is worth it for the majority of users or should
be exposed as a configuration knob, as GCC does with
<tt>-fwrapv</tt>.

So how did this happen? Why has undefined behavior become so
strangely understood? I suspect part of the explanation is
economic. Historically, compiler vendors competed directly
for paying compiler users. Today, most compiler development 
is funded by organizations with substantial interests in 
hardware and platform performance. That creates incentives 
to prioritize optimization opportunities, including those 
enabled by undefined behavior and has weakened the pressure
to preserve useful behavior in programs that don't
strictly conform to the standard.

As a user of the C programming language, undefined behavior
in your source program makes it non-portable: it might work
on your compiler today, but other compilers might not
choose to preserve useful semantics. Other compilers might
even be a different version of your compiler, or even the
same compiler if you change the source of the program and
trigger some different analysis. As a result you should
avoid it as best you can. But how the compiler chooses to
act in the face of undefined behavior is not because the C
standard says to do that: your compiler chose to do that.
::raw
  </article>
